<DANMILLER> Copyright Daniel Miller April 88
<DANMILLER> The following is the HAL PC Newsletter May Forth Column:
<DANMILLER> HAL is the Houston Area League of PC Users Group
<DANMILLER> and this is posted on BIX just for general interest although the
<DANMILLER> content is probably old hat to BIX forthies...
<DANMILLER> Forth Notes:
<DANMILLER>      First, thanks to all who helped man the Forth Sig table at the
<DANMILLER> HAL PC annual convention.  I will not list names for fear of leaving
<DANMILLER> someone out.  But we all had a good time and put on a nice 
presentation.
<DANMILLER> We had  displays of Forth software and forth compilers, hardware
<DANMILLER> systems and a Nasa video application coded in Forth.  This was a 
significant
<DANMILLER> amount of work for the volunteers involved.
<DANMILLER>       The Forth sig now has a conference area on the HAL board
<DANMILLER> Telcom 1.  This is the place to ask questions on how to get started,
<DANMILLER> availablity of public domain compilers for the ibm pc, discussion of
<DANMILLER> relative merits of commercial compilers and to find
<DANMILLER> out something about Forth syntax and the various Forth textbooks.  
There
<DANMILLER> is a listing applications coded in Forth.
<DANMILLER>      Some recent Forth developments include release of a super z8 
microcomputer
<DANMILLER> which supports instructions for fast forth and is available on 
demonstrator
<DANMILLER> a card for $88 and has an available Forth stand alone prom for 
under $100.
<DANMILLER> Other Forth processors include the Motorola F68hc11 controller cpu 
which
<DANMILLER> has an on chip a/d converter.  We have Forth Sig members doing forth
<DANMILLER> commercial projects in Houston using these cpu's.
<DANMILLER>      I have seen a parallel PC system in Silicon Valley using Forth 
RISC chips
<DANMILLER> capable of running 6 boards in a pc chassis with each board having 
its own
<DANMILLER> memory and capable of independent operation once initialized by the 
host pc.
<DANMILLER> Output can be directed to various windows on the pc screen which 
can be
<DANMILLER> dragged around by a mouse. Speed for integer operations and control 
logic is
<DANMILLER> fast and 6 board system can be purchased at a claimed cost per 
board about
<DANMILLER> half that of a transputer node.
<DANMILLER>      Goddard Space Labs has a project programming a massively 
parallel
<DANMILLER> computer in Forth (MPP project). Is anyone in Houston connecting 
cpu's
<DANMILLER> directly with FIFO's and cross point switches? Is anyone here doing 
any
<DANMILLER> parallel development or neural networking.  Let us know on Telcom 
one- join
<DANMILLER> forth.
<DANMILLER>      I also saw running atop the Coastal Ridge above Palo Alto 
overlooking
<DANMILLER> Half Moon Bay, a low cost prototype of a very
<DANMILLER> interesting programming environment using only three keys.  These 
keys are
<DANMILLER> pressed in binary combination to select 7 items.  The key 
combination is
<DANMILLER> pressed and the computer recognizes the key combination when the 
keys are held
<DANMILLER> stable for a moment.   Since in Forth all words in a definition 
except the
<DANMILLER> first name are predefined, these can be quickly picked from pages 
of the
<DANMILLER> dictionary of defined words. The unit I saw used a one inch one 
megabyte
<DANMILLER> floppy disc and had one 4 by 6" card sitting on top of the drive.  
The 3 keys
<DANMILLER> plugged into the card and the cable to a Sony multisynch color 
monitor plugged
<DANMILLER> also into the card.  Color no less, and an integer and control 
logic compute
<DANMILLER> speed faster than an 80386 20 mhz machine.  My first thought was 
hey this is
<DANMILLER> crazy, how do you input names and addresses.  Well you could pick 
each letter
<DANMILLER> using three strokes of the keyboard or pick names from menus or 
frequent
<DANMILLER> letter combinations.  A little awkward.  But then, do all computers 
use name
<DANMILLER> or address type input?  What about control computers, things that 
turn pumps
<DANMILLER> on or off, or control rockets or steering mechanisms or robots.  
These don't
<DANMILLER> need any more than three key menu systems.  Then I thought of all 
the people
<DANMILLER> who can't type --a lot of important people- like my wife and most 
of the rest
<DANMILLER> of the world's population.  The machine began to have more appeal. 
Besides its
<DANMILLER> intended use as a programming station and a programming environment 
it could
<DANMILLER> have quite a bit of general purpose application. And it could be 
mass produced
<DANMILLER> inexpensively having very little actual silicon or metal contacts, 
minimal
<DANMILLER> power supply and yet is powerful, contains a programming and 
application
<DANMILLER> environment and pertinent to this column, runs Forth, "real fast." 
Contact
<DANMILLER> Computer Cowboys 410 Star Hill Road, Woodside, CA 94062 telephone 
(415) 851
<DANMILLER> 4362 for preproduction information and to express an interest.
<DANMILLER>                                                 dlm
